          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-body">
        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">Weight-loss publishing splits into four tiers, and where you can land a byline depends almost entirely on the credential behind it.</p>
            <p>The medically-reviewed brands do not take open guest posts. The programme media take evidence-based nutrition. The recipe sites take genuine meal content. The coaching blogs are open. The list of 100 is below, grouped by tier.</p>

            <h2 id="tier">Pick your tier first</h2>
            <figure><table>
              <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Examples</th><th>How you get in</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Medically-reviewed health brands</td><td>Healthline, WebMD, Medical News Today, Verywell Fit, Everyday Health, Prevention</td><td>Not open guest posts. A byline from a registered dietitian, physician or licensed professional</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Diet programmes &amp; nutrition media</td><td>WeightWatchers, Noom, Diet Doctor, Eating Well, Precision Nutrition, Nutrisystem</td><td>Evidence-based nutrition pieces, protocol explainers, myth-busting from qualified authors</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Recipe &amp; meal-plan sites</td><td>Skinnytaste, Slender Kitchen, The Diet Chefs, Healthy Fitness Meals, Diethood</td><td>Genuine recipes with nutrition data, meal-plan frameworks, batch-cooking guides</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Coaching &amp; results blogs</td><td>Fat-Burning Man, Living Lean, Fit Watch, Tone It Up, Power of Positivity Health</td><td>Real client results with data, sustainable-habit content, honest expectation-setting</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table></figure>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <figure><table>
              <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table></figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> weight loss is core YMYL and one of the most tightly policed topics in search. Google weighs named, credentialed authors and medical review heavily, and disordered-eating safeguards matter. A link earned with a real RD or MD byline is in a different class.</p></div>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority, URL Rating and estimated monthly traffic. Every domain was checked live in September 2026; shut-down and redirected sites were replaced with live equivalents. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time.</p>
            <figure><table>
              <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th><th>Monthly traffic</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {SITES.map((s) => (
                  <tr><td>{s.n}</td><td>{s.site}</td><td>{s.dr}</td><td>{s.da}</td><td>{s.pa}</td><td>{s.ur}</td><td>{s.traffic}</td></tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table></figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Lead with the credential and the evidence.</strong> "As a registered dietitian, here is what the latest research on protein and satiety actually shows" earns the read.</li>
              <li><strong>Cite primary sources.</strong> Link the studies, not a summary of a summary.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the section.</strong> "For your nutrition coverage" or "for the sustainable-habits series" shows you read the site.</li>
              <li><strong>Frame it responsibly.</strong> Moderate approach, realistic timelines, no before-and-after shock tactics.</li>
              <li><strong>Bring the assets.</strong> A protocol, a meal framework, a data set.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose any programme, app or product</strong> you have a stake in.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would an editor run this if you had nothing to sell? If yes, pitch it. If it exists only to place a link, it goes in the bin.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Pitch a "lose weight fast" listicle with no author credential.</li>
              <li>Make metabolism or supplement claims you cannot back with a study.</li>
              <li>Use dramatic before-and-after framing or extreme-deficit advice.</li>
              <li>Put exact-match anchors ("best weight loss pill") on links back to your store.</li>
              <li>Use AI-generated diet content. In a YMYL niche it is both obvious and risky.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> a few placements on medically-reviewed sites with a real dietitian or physician byline will do more for rankings and trust than a long list of thin diet-blog links.</p></div>
          </div>

          <aside class="article-toc">
            <h4>On this page</h4>
            <ul>{toc.map((item) => (<li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.text}</a></li>))}</ul>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div id="faq"><Faq items={faqs} /></div>

